@startuml
!include style.puml
title A state change reaching an app

participant "Home Assistant" as ha $ha
participant "Broker" as b $broker
participant "Worker 0" as w0 $worker
participant "Worker 1" as w1 $worker
participant "App\n(in worker 0)" as app $usercode

ha -> b : event state_changed\n{ entity_id, old_state, new_state }
b -> b : StateCache.applyChange()

par fan-out to every worker
    b -> w0 : **StateChangeBatch** { changes }
    b -> w1 : **StateChangeBatch** { changes }
end

w0 -> w0 : stateCache.applyChange()
w1 -> w1 : stateCache.applyChange()
w0 -> w0 : match subscriptions\n**memoised** per entity id
note over w1
  Worker 1 still updates its state cache even
  though nothing there subscribed. A full
  state cache can only stay fresh if it sees
  **every** change — matching decides who
  gets //told//, never who gets //updated//.
end note

w0 -> app : handler(StateChange)  <<own fiber>>
note right of app
  Each subscription drains its queue in its
  own fiber, so a handler that awaits a service
  call holds back only its own subscription.
end note
@enduml
